
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: Role
metadata:
  name: cessda-developer
  namespace: cessda-mgmt
rules:
  - apiGroups: [""]
    resources: ["pods", "pods/log", "services", "configmaps"]
    verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]
  - apiGroups: ["apps"]
    resources: ["deployments", "replicasets", "statefulsets"]
    verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]
  - apiGroups: [""]
    resources: ["pods/exec"]
    verbs: ["create"]



apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: RoleBinding
metadata:
  name: cessda-developer-binding
  namespace: cessda-mgmt
subjects:
  - kind: User
    name: developer@company.com
    apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io
roleRef:
  kind: Role
  name: cessda-developer
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io

kubectl apply -f cessda-developer-role.yaml
kubectl apply -f cessda-developer-binding.yaml

kubectl auth can-i list pods \
  -n cessda-mgmt \
  --as=developer@company.com

kubectl auth can-i create deployments \
  -n cessda-mgmt \
  --as=developer@company.com

Option A — Individual login through OIDC/SSO
The developer receives the cluster endpoint and CA information, configures their own kubeconfig and authenticates using the company's identity provider. This is my preferred long-term approach.
Option B — Temporary access
Issue a short-lived credential through a supported authentication mechanism and provide a kubeconfig configured for that credential. Ensure it is revocable or expires as intended and has only the required RBAC permissions.
A critical distinction: kubectl create token creates a token for a ServiceAccount, not an individual developer. If you use this method, create a dedicated ServiceAccount with narrowly scoped permissions, understand the audit implications, and do not share a token belonging to an administrator or application with broader privileges.
For kubeconfig concepts and safe configuration, see the Kubernetes documentation.

